  "configuration_id": "config_030",
  "reason": "Evidence: config_018 (multimodal, multiprofile, pca_n2000, resnet50, avgpool, concat) is the best result at 0.619 acc, and RNA signal is the dominant driver (RNA-only multiprofile avgpool reaches 0.591, while image-only is only 0.208). The only RNA encoder tested so far is the simple linear PCA (pca_n2000). Question: Does a learned nonlinear RNA representation (SCVI) improve over PCA in the strongest configuration, holding all other design choices (multiprofile, avgpool, resnet50, concat) constant? Why this is the best next experiment: It isolates the RNA encoder effect in the highest-performing regime without changing multiple variables simultaneously. If SCVI improves performance, it identifies the RNA representation as a key lever for further gains; if it does not, it tells us PCA already captures the relevant variance and we should look elsewhere (e.g., image encoder or fusion). This is more informative than testing weighted fusion (config_019), where the singleprofile evidence (0.527 vs 0.523) suggests only a marginal difference, or testing a new image encoder when image-only performance is so weak that the image contribution appears limited."
}

